// The skinmesh package: a quad mesh grown from a graph of nodes and edges,
// with a rig from the same graph. The graph is a stick figure (a position and a
// radius, across x and across z, at each node); the skin is the creature's base
// mesh, ready to subdivide and to sculpt, and the armature and weights make it
// move. It is Blender's Skin modifier and ZBrush's ZSpheres (Adaptive Skin),
// built here as follows.
//
//   import skinmesh use *;
//   mut g = SkinGraph.empty();
//   imm hip = g.addNode(Vec3(0., 1., 0.), 0.18, 0.12);
//   imm chest = g.grow(hip, Vec3(0., 1.5, 0.), 0.22, 0.13);
//   ...
//   imm s = skinStandard(&g);          // s.mesh, s.rig, s.weights, s.problems
//   imm fine = subdivideSkin(&s, 2u32);
//
// What the skin is
// - A node of degree one is an end: a ring (a rectangle of half-extents radiusX
//   and radiusZ, square to the edge) closed by one quad, or with SkinCapDome by
//   a smaller ring beyond it and then a quad.
// - A node of degree two is a joint. A smooth one (the default) has one ring in
//   the plane that bisects the bend, widened along the bend by 1 / cos of half
//   the turn (up to 'mitreLimit') so the tube keeps its thickness, as a mitre
//   joint does; the two edges' tubes meet at that one ring. A loose one
//   (SkinLoose, or an edge folded back on its neighbour) keeps a ring of its
//   own for each edge, a little way out along it, and a band across the joint:
//   the corner stays sharper, and after subdivision the joint is a knuckle.
// - A node of degree three or more is a branch. Each edge gets a ring square to
//   it, standing off from the node by the least distance that makes every ring
//   a face of the convex hull of all the rings' corners (the fixed point of
//   t_i = max over the other rings' corners p of p . q_i, which is about
//   r / tan(theta / 2) for two edges theta apart), plus 'smooth' radii at a
//   smooth node; a loose branch keeps the least. The hull's other faces are the
//   junction: neighbouring triangles merged into quads where the four corners
//   are near a plane, and the rest left (counted in Skin.triangles).
// - A node of degree zero is a box.
// - The tubes between rings are quads, with 'cuts' extra rings between, and no
//   twist: the corner of one ring met by the other's is the nearest.
//
// Frames: each edge is oriented away from its tree's root (a node marked
// SkinRoot, else the tree's lowest-numbered node). A ring's x axis is the start
// node's hint (SkinGraph.setHint) if it has one, else the parent edge's x axis
// turned with the least rotation (Quat.fromVectors) to this edge, else the world
// x (or z). So a limb keeps its flat side to the same side all along, and a
// side branch inherits it.
//
// Channels: vertex "along" (0 to 1 from the start of the vertex's limb, a run
// of edges through smooth or loose joints between branches, ends and the root,
// by distance), "around" (the ring's corner, 0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4), "limb" (the
// limb's number, as f32) and the corner "uv" (u around, v along, 1 at the seam
// of a tube; at a junction or a cap the vertex's own values: not unwrapped).
// Subdivision carries them as sculpt's subdivide carries any channel.
//
// The rig: one bone for each edge (Armature), numbered parents first, head at
// the edge's start node, tail at its end; 'skeleton' gives the same as
// bonepose's Skeleton. The weights (SkinWeights, creaturerig's layout) are two
// bones at most on the base mesh: a vertex of a ring that two bones share
// (a smooth joint's, a branch's child edge's ring with its parent) is half and
// half; a ring that one edge owns is 1; the rings cut into a tube blend
// linearly from half and half at a shared end to 1 at the middle. After
// subdivision the weights are carried vertex by vertex, as channels, and the
// best four kept.
//
// The algorithms are from the literature; parameters follow Blender's manual,
// never its source:
// - the skin of a graph of nodes with radii and the hull at a branch: Blender's
//   Skin modifier (the manual gives its options: root, loose, radius, branch
//   smoothing) and, as a method, Ji, Liu, Wang and Yang (2010), "B-Mesh: A
//   modeling system for base meshes of 3D articulated shapes", Computer Graphics
//   Forum 29(7): a joint is a convex hull of the connecting tubes' ends,
//   quadrangulated; Bærentzen et al. (2012), "Two methods for skinning
//   branching structures": the same idea with a quad-dominant hull;
// - rotation-minimizing frames along a tree: Wang, Juttler, Zheng and Liu
//   (2008), as sculpt's paths use them;
// - the hull of a few points by supporting planes over all triples, and
//   triangles merged into quads by a cheapest-first matching with augmenting
//   paths (the dual of the hull's triangles as the graph; not blossom, so an
//   odd cycle may leave a triangle).
//
// Its conventions are sculpt's: right-handed space, y up, f32, faces wound
// counterclockwise seen from outside.
//
//   graph.cone    SkinGraph: nodes, radii, edges, flags (root, loose), hints
//   hull.cone     convexHull3 (supporting planes), quadrangulate (triangle pairs)
//   build.cone    SkinOptions, Skin, and the Builder: orientation from the root,
//                 limbs, rings, tubes, caps, joints and junctions
//   rig.cone      Armature, SkinWeights, the weights as channels and back
//   skin.cone     skin, skinStandard, subdivideSkin: the whole build

mod skinmesh;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use *;
import bonepose use *;
import sculpt use *;
